        "title": "The First Toolmakers",
        "reading": "More than two and a half million years ago, in what is now Tanzania, someone picked up a stone and struck it against another rock. Sharp flakes broke away. That act changed the world. The broken stone could cut meat, scrape wood, and crush plants. Scientists call these makers hominins, early members of the human family tree. Their oldest known tools, called Oldowan tools, were found in a valley named Olduvai Gorge.\n\nThe discoveries at Olduvai Gorge proved something powerful: humanity began in Africa. In the 1960s, archaeologist Mary Leakey uncovered some of the oldest tools ever found, buried beside the bones of the hominins who made them. These tools were simple, but they were not accidents. The maker chose the right stone and struck it at the right angle. An archaeologist's field note from a dig like this might read: \"June 12. I worked the east trench all morning. I found a stone with flakes struck from both sides. The edge is still sharp. This stone did not break by chance. Someone shaped it on purpose.\"\n\nToolmaking set our ancestors apart from every other animal. No other creature shapes stone tools and teaches the skill to its young. Sharp flakes let early humans cut meat from animals they could never catch with bare hands. Better food fed bigger brains, and bigger brains made better tools. It was a slow cycle that built the human mind. Every computer and spaceship traces back to that first struck stone in Africa.",
