        "title": "Seeds of Change",
        "reading": "For most of human history, people survived by hunting animals and gathering wild plants. Then, about ten thousand years ago, everything changed. In the hills of the Middle East, people began planting seeds and tending the young plants. They also tamed wild sheep and goats. This great change is called the Agricultural Revolution. It happened in many places at once. In Africa, farmers domesticated sorghum, millet, and yams, crops that still feed millions of people today.\n\nFarming had winners and losers, and two ancient voices might describe it this way. A farmer could say: \"My fields give me grain every year. I no longer wander. My children eat even in winter.\" A hunter might answer: \"You labor from sunrise to sunset in the hot field. I walk free and eat meat, nuts, and berries. Your back is bent, but mine is straight.\" Both had a point. Farmers worked longer hours and faced new diseases from crowded villages and farm animals.\n\nYet farming produced something hunting never could: a surplus. Extra grain could be stored, traded, or given to workers who did not farm. With a surplus, some people became potters, priests, builders, and rulers. Villages grew into towns, and towns grew into cities. The Agricultural Revolution did not just change dinner. It changed how humans lived, worked, and governed one another, in the Middle East, in Africa, and around the world.",
