        "title": "Cities Rise Between the Rivers",
        "reading": "Between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers lay a flat, dry plain with rich soil but little rain. Around 4000 BCE, the people there solved the problem with irrigation. They dug canals that carried river water to their fields of barley and wheat. The harvests were huge. For the first time, a farming village could feed thousands of people, and the village of Uruk swelled into the world's first city.\n\nUruk was a city-state, an independent city ruling the farmland around it. Its streets held potters, weavers, priests, and kings. Its traders sailed south to the Persian Gulf and west toward distant lands. Far to the southwest, the African kingdom of Nubia was rising too, and Nubian traders carried gold and ivory north along routes that linked Africa to Mesopotamia. The first cities did not grow alone. They grew through trade.\n\nCities needed rules, and the most famous came from King Hammurabi of Babylon. His law code was carved on stone for all to see: \"If a man damages another man's field, he shall pay for the lost grain. If a judge changes his verdict for silver, he shall be removed from his seat. The law stands above every citizen.\" The code was harsh by our standards, but it announced a new idea: even the powerful must answer to written law.",
