        "title": "Mountains for the Dead",
        "reading": "The pyramids were mountains built for the dead. Egyptians believed the pharaoh became a god after death, so his tomb had to be worthy of a god. At Giza, three great pyramids rise from the desert, the largest built for Pharaoh Khufu around 2560 BCE. It held more than two million stone blocks and stood as the tallest building on Earth for nearly four thousand years.\n\nBuilding a pyramid took an army of workers, not slaves as old stories claimed. Archaeologists have found the workers' villages, bakeries, and graves near Giza. Crews dragged limestone blocks on sleds over wet sand and raised them up ramps of mud brick. Each block was cut to fit its neighbors almost perfectly. The pyramid was a machine for showing power: look what the pharaoh can command.\n\nThe pyramids still speak. Their perfect alignment with the stars shows Egyptian knowledge of math and astronomy. Their size shows a government strong enough to feed and organize tens of thousands of workers. When you stand before the Great Pyramid, you are reading a message carved in stone: here ruled a king who believed death was only the beginning.\n\nThe pyramid age did not last. Later pharaohs hid their tombs in secret valleys to foil robbers, but the great pyramids at Giza could not be hidden or matched. They remain the loudest statement any king ever made in stone.",
