        "title": "A Day on the Nile",
        "reading": "Most Egyptians never saw the inside of a palace. They were farmers who rose with the sun, waded into the fields, and cut wheat with curved sickles. When the Nile flooded, they repaired canals and built tombs for the pharaoh. Their bread was coarse, their beer was thick, and their lives followed the river's rhythm: flood, planting, harvest, repeat.\n\nAbove the farmers stood the scribes and artisans. Scribes trained for years to master hieroglyphs, then recorded taxes, harvests, and royal orders. Artisans shaped gold, carved stone, and painted tomb walls with scenes of feasts and hunts. One of Egypt's greatest minds was Imhotep, an African architect and physician who designed the first pyramid, the Step Pyramid, and wrote some of the earliest known medical texts.\n\nAn Egyptian worker might write home like this: \"Dear brother, the harvest was good this year. The overseer gave me extra grain for the tomb work. Mother is well, and the baby walks now. Send word when the tax collector passes.\" Life along the Nile was hard, but it was organized, skilled, and deeply human. The farmers, scribes, and artisans built a civilization that lasted three thousand years.\n\nThree thousand years later, the Nile still floods, farmers still plant, and scribes, now called clerks, still keep the records. The tools changed. The rhythm remains.",
