        "title": "Gold of the Desert",
        "reading": "South of the Sahara Desert lay a kingdom rich in gold. In the 1200s, a leader named Sundiata united the peoples of the region into the Mali Empire. Its wealth came from trade: camel caravans carried salt south from the desert and gold north to the Mediterranean. The king taxed every load that crossed his land, and the gold piled up in his treasuries.\n\nMali's most famous king was Mansa Musa, who ruled in the 1300s. He was likely the richest man in history. In 1324, he made a pilgrimage to Mecca, the holy city of Islam, traveling with thousands of attendants and camels loaded with gold. Along the way he gave away so much gold that prices in Egypt fell for years. The world had never seen wealth like it.\n\nA traveler named Ibn Battuta visited Mali years later and wrote: \"The people of Mali are generous and honor their guests. Their king gives gifts of gold to poets and scholars. In the markets, I saw salt traded for its weight in gold.\" His account shows an empire known not only for riches but for welcome. Mansa Musa's journey put Africa's wealth and faith on the world's map.\n\nMansa Musa died around 1337, but the stories never stopped. Maps in Europe began marking Mali with a golden king on a golden throne. The world had learned that Africa held empires to rival any on Earth.",
