        "title": "Athens and Sparta",
        "reading": "To understand Greece, compare its two giants side by side. Athens was a busy port where merchants, artists, and thinkers crowded the marketplace. Its assembly let thousands of citizens vote on war and peace. Sparta was a walled camp where life served the army. Spartan men lived in barracks, ate plain food, and trained for battle from childhood.\n\nEach city paid a price for its choice. Athens's love of debate sometimes led to mob rule, and its assembly once voted to execute its own best generals in anger. Sparta's discipline made it strong in war but poor in art, science, and new ideas. For two hundred years the two cities fought for control of Greece, and their rivalry finally tore the Greek world apart.\n\nNeither city was a fair place by modern eyes. In both, enslaved people did the hard labor. In both, women could not vote. The Greek experiment with citizen power was real, but it was narrow. It would take two thousand years before the idea of rule by the people grew to include everyone.\n\nThe rivalry ended badly for both. Sparta beat Athens in a long war, then Thebes beat Sparta, then Macedonia conquered them all. The poleis proved brilliant at competing and terrible at uniting. Their ideas outlived their armies, which may be the real victory.",
