        "title": "From Republic to Empire",
        "reading": "After kings ruled Rome badly, the Romans tried something new. Around 509 BCE they founded a republic, a government with no king. Citizens elected two consuls to lead for one year, and a Senate of elders advised them. Laws were written down in the Twelve Tables so every citizen could read them. One table read: \"If a person breaks another's bone, let there be a penalty. The law is the same for rich and poor.\"\n\nThe republic's strength was its army. Roman legions, units of about 5,000 soldiers, trained harder than any army on Earth. They marched twenty miles a day, built a fortified camp every night, and never broke formation. With these legions, Rome conquered Italy, then the Mediterranean, then lands from Britain to Syria.\n\nRome's empire reached lands far south of the Mediterranean too. In 193 CE, a general named Septimius Severus became emperor. He was born in Leptis Magna, in what is now Libya, and he never lost his African accent. He ruled the entire Roman world for eighteen years, built great monuments, and raised his sons to follow him. A boy from Africa had climbed to the top of the world's greatest empire.\n\nThe republic's ideals did not die with it. For two thousand years, people fighting kings have pointed to Rome and said: citizens can rule themselves. The Senate's marble is dust, but its idea marches on.",
