        "title": "DNA: The Blueprint of Life",
        "reading": "Inside the nucleus of every cell sits the blueprint for a whole living thing. That blueprint is DNA, a long molecule shaped like a twisted ladder. The rungs of the ladder are pairs of chemicals called bases, and their order spells out instructions. Those instructions build you: your height, your hair, the color of your eyes.\n\nDNA is packed into structures called chromosomes. Humans have 46 chromosomes in each cell, 23 from each parent. Stretched out, the DNA in one cell would be about six feet long, yet it fits inside a nucleus smaller than a dust speck. It is the densest instruction manual in nature.\n\nSections of DNA called genes carry the instructions for single traits, like Mendel's pea colors. When a cell divides, it copies its entire DNA so each new cell gets the full blueprint. Every living thing, from bacteria to blue whales, runs on DNA.\n\nHere is a wonder to carry with you: the DNA in your body, laid end to end, would stretch to the sun and back many times. All of it fits inside cells too small to see. Nature writes its longest books in its tiniest print. Read carefully: you are the story it tells.",
