        "title": "All Roads Lead to Rome",
        "reading": "Rome's power rested on roads. At its height, the empire held more than 50,000 miles of paved highways, built by legions with layers of stone, gravel, and sand. A famous saying claimed all roads lead to Rome, and it was nearly true: a traveler could walk from Britain to Syria without leaving a Roman road. Armies, mail, and grain moved fast because the roads were straight, drained, and guarded.\n\nWater moved just as surely. Roman aqueducts carried fresh water across valleys on towering arches, dropping only a few feet per mile so the flow never stopped. Rome's eleven aqueducts delivered enough water for a million people, feeding fountains, baths, and homes. The Pont du Gard in France still stands today, a bridge of stone carrying a river of water.\n\nThese works served everyone, and they served justice too. Centuries later, the American lawyer Thurgood Marshall used law the way Romans used roads: as a path to reach people. As a civil rights lawyer and then a Supreme Court justice, Marshall argued that the law must protect every citizen equally. Rome built roads of stone. Marshall helped build roads of justice.\n\nMany Roman roads are still in use, paved over by modern highways. Drivers in England and France travel Roman routes every day without knowing it. The empire fell, but its roads refused to retire.",
