        "title": "The Empire That Would Not Die",
        "reading": "When Rome fell in the west, it lived on in the east. The city of Constantinople, built on the strait between Europe and Asia, became the capital of what we call the Byzantine Empire. It held the richest markets in the world, guarded by massive walls and a golden chain across its harbor. While western Europe forgot how to read, Byzantium kept its libraries full.\n\nThe Byzantines spoke of their faith in glittering mosaics. In the church of San Vitale stands a famous mosaic of Emperor Justinian, robed in purple, holding a golden bowl, flanked by soldiers and priests. Every tile was placed to send a message: the emperor rules by God's will, the church and state stand as one, and this empire will not fall. A mosaic was a sermon in stone.\n\nFar to the south, another Christian civilization carved its faith in rock. In Ethiopia, King Lalibela ordered eleven churches cut straight down into solid stone, complete with doors, windows, and roofs, all carved from one piece of rock. Workers dug from the top down, freeing each church from the mountain. These medieval African wonders still stand, and pilgrims still worship in them today.\n\nConstantinople's walls held for a thousand years against every attacker. Its libraries preserved books the west had lost. When we call it Byzantine, we should hear the word with respect: it was Rome, refusing to die.",
