        "reading": "In 762 CE, the caliph al-Mansur built a new capital: Baghdad, a round city on the Tigris River. A few decades later, his successors founded the House of Wisdom, a great library and research center. Scholars there translated Greek, Persian, and Indian works into Arabic, then went beyond them. For five hundred years, Baghdad was the center of world science.\n\nThe achievements were stunning. Al-Khwarizmi invented algebra, giving math a new language for solving problems. Ibn Sina wrote a medical encyclopedia used in Europe for six hundred years. Scholars mapped the stars and built the first hospitals. A scholar of that age wrote: \"Knowledge must be tested, not only believed. We learn the states of the body by watching and by reason. The astrolabe shows us the heavens, and careful study shows us the truth.\"\n\nThe golden age reached Africa too. In Timbuktu, Muslim scholars copied and studied the works of Baghdad, building libraries of their own. African and Arab learning flowed together along the desert trade routes. When Baghdad fell in 1258, its books lived on in cities like Timbuktu, where families still guard the manuscripts today.\n\nThe House of Wisdom burned when Baghdad fell, but its method survived. Translate, test, and share: that three-word recipe still runs every laboratory on Earth. Baghdad's library is gone. Its idea built the modern world.",
