        "title": "Life on the Manor",
        "reading": "Most people in medieval Europe never saw a king. They lived on a manor, a self-sufficient estate run by a lord. At the center stood the manor house or a small castle. Around it lay farmland, meadows for animals, forests for firewood, and small cottages where peasant families lived. Almost everything the people needed was made or grown on the manor, so villages rarely depended on faraway markets.\n\nThe peasants called serfs were bound to the land. They could not leave without the lord's permission, and they farmed his fields several days each week before working their own small strips. They paid rent in crops, animals, and labor. In exchange, the lord gave them protection when raiders came and a place in his court where disputes were settled. Life was hard and food was simple, but the manor gave peasants a secure place in an uncertain world.\n\nThe bond between lord and vassal was personal, sealed by an oath spoken aloud. One such oath, recorded in medieval France, reads: \"I promise by my faith that I will be faithful to my lord. I will never harm him, and I will defend him with all my power. So help me God.\" The words sound like a contract, and that is what they were. Spoken promises held the whole system together. Whether spoken in a French castle or sung by a Malian griot, a person's word carried the weight of law.",
