        "title": "Castles: Medieval Super-Technology",
        "reading": "A medieval castle was the most advanced technology of its day, part fortress and part statement of power. Early castles were simple wooden towers on mounds of earth, but attackers learned to burn them down. Builders switched to stone, raising thick walls, tall towers, and a massive central tower called the keep. Every feature had a purpose. A moat slowed attackers before they reached the walls. A drawbridge could be raised to seal the gate. Arrow slits let defenders shoot while staying hidden, and openings above gates let them drop stones on enemies below.\n\nLater castles used concentric walls, one ring inside another, so breaking through the outer wall only led to another wall. Castles were also built to impress. Grand halls, fine carvings, and towering walls told every visitor that the lord was powerful and not to be challenged. Building a great castle took years and the labor of hundreds of workers, which is why only the richest lords could afford the finest ones.\n\nCastle building was not only a European art. In Spain, the Moors, Muslim rulers from North Africa, built magnificent fortresses like the Alhambra, blending strong defenses with beautiful design. Moorish scholars also preserved and advanced the science and mathematics that later helped European engineers. Like a well-designed machine, a great castle combined strength, beauty, and clever engineering in one structure.",
