        "title": "Knights: Warriors with a Code",
        "reading": "Knights were the armored warriors of medieval Europe, and becoming one took years of training. A boy of seven might become a page, serving in a lord's household and learning manners. Around fourteen he became a squire, caring for a knight's armor and horses and practicing with real weapons. If he proved brave and loyal, he was dubbed a knight in a ceremony with a sword tapped on each shoulder.\n\nKnights were expected to follow chivalry, a code of behavior that prized courage, loyalty, honesty, and protection of the weak. In stories, knights always lived up to this code. In real life, some did and some did not, but the ideal shaped how nobles were supposed to behave. Tournaments gave knights a place to practice fighting and win glory without going to war.\n\nWriters described castles very differently depending on their purpose. A medieval visitor wrote: \"The walls are thick stone, three times the height of a man. The keep rises above them all, and armed guards watch the gates day and night.\" A modern historian wrote: \"The castle was built to impress visitors as much as to stop attackers. Its grand halls showed the lord's wealth and power.\" The first writer sees a fortress. The second sees a message. Comparing the two accounts shows that the same castle can tell more than one story.",
