        "title": "The Crusades Up Close: A Crusader's Letter",
        "reading": "History feels different when you read the words of someone who lived it. Crusaders on the long march to Jerusalem wrote letters home describing hunger, fear, and hope. One such letter reads: \"We have marched many miles across dry land. Our food is nearly gone, but we press on toward Jerusalem. Pray for me, for I do not know if I will see home again.\"\n\nThree short sentences carry a whole story. The writer is exhausted and hungry, yet he keeps marching because his faith drives him forward. He asks for prayers, which shows he fears he might die. Letters like this remind us that the Crusades were not just battles on a map. They were made of real people who missed their families and wondered if they would survive.\n\nLife on crusade was brutal. Armies marched through deserts with little water. Disease killed more soldiers than battles did. When cities fell, terrible violence followed on both sides. Yet moments of respect broke through the fighting. Christian and Muslim leaders sometimes negotiated truces, exchanged gifts, and admired each other's courage. Reading a crusader's letter helps us see the human being inside the armor, a person caught between devotion and doubt on a road far from home.",
