        "title": "Pax Mongolica: Peace, Trade, and Tolerance",
        "reading": "After the conquests came an unexpected peace. Historians call it the Pax Mongolica, the Mongol Peace. Because one power controlled the trade routes from China to Europe, merchants could travel the Silk Road in relative safety. The Mongols protected traders, set up relay stations with fresh horses every few miles, and kept the roads clear of bandits. Ideas, inventions, and goods flowed across Asia as never before.\n\nThe Mongols were also surprisingly tolerant of religion. Genghis Khan welcomed Buddhists, Christians, Muslims, and others at his court, and his successors let each people keep their own faiths and customs as long as they paid taxes and obeyed the law. This tolerance was practical: it kept a giant, diverse empire from rebelling. A merchant traveling under Mongol protection wrote: \"The Great Khan's court is grander than any in Europe. Messengers ride day and night along safe roads. A traveler may journey for weeks and find food and shelter at every post station.\"\n\nRead that account closely. The merchant praises safety, speed, and comfort, the exact things traders need. He is not describing battles but infrastructure: roads, stations, and law. The Mongol Empire shows that power can be used for more than war. By guarding the roads and tolerating differences, the khans connected the world and helped ideas like printing and the compass travel west to Europe.",
