        "title": "Humans and Ecosystems",
        "reading": "Humans are the most powerful force shaping ecosystems today. Our actions change habitats faster than any natural process. When forests are cleared for farms or cities, the species living there lose their homes. This habitat destruction is the leading cause of extinction worldwide. Pollution adds another blow: plastics fill the oceans, chemicals poison rivers, and exhaust thickens the air.\n\nOur choices ripple through food webs. Overfishing has emptied parts of the ocean that once teemed with life. When top predators disappear, prey populations explode and then crash, throwing whole ecosystems off balance. Introducing non-native species causes similar chaos. Rabbits released in Australia, with no natural predators, multiplied into the hundreds of millions and devoured native plants.\n\nClimate change is the largest human impact of all. Burning coal, oil, and gas releases greenhouse gases that trap heat, warming the planet. Coral reefs bleach, glaciers melt, and storms grow stronger. But understanding our impact is the first step to reducing it. Scientists measure these changes precisely so leaders and citizens can act on facts. Every plastic bottle and every gallon of gas leaves a mark on the living world. The same curiosity that drove Renaissance thinkers to study the world now drives ecologists to protect it.",
