        "title": "Luther and the Reformation",
        "reading": "In 1517, a German monk named Martin Luther challenged the most powerful institution in Europe: the Catholic Church. Luther believed the church had become corrupt, especially in selling indulgences, payments people made to reduce punishment for sins. He wrote out 95 theses, or arguments, and according to tradition nailed them to the door of a church in Wittenberg. He wanted debate, but he got a revolution.\n\nThe timing made all the difference. The printing press, invented decades earlier, could copy Luther's writings by the thousands. His ideas spread across Germany in weeks and across Europe in years. Ordinary people could now read the Bible in their own languages instead of Latin. Luther's challenge split Western Christianity into Catholics and Protestants, and the split led to more than a century of religious wars.\n\nChristianity's story is wider than Europe, though. In Ethiopia, an ancient Christian church had thrived since the 300s, independent of Rome. Ethiopian Christians built rock-carved churches, kept sacred texts in their own ancient language, and practiced traditions older than many European kingdoms. While Europe's Christians were splitting apart, Ethiopia's church quietly preserved some of the oldest Christian traditions on Earth. The Reformation reminds us that faith, like history, has more than one center.",
