        "title": "The Age of Exploration Begins",
        "reading": "In the 1400s, Europeans began sailing into the unknown, launching the Age of Exploration. Several motives pushed them. They wanted Asian spices, silk, and gold, which traveled overland through expensive middlemen. They wanted to spread Christianity. And new technology made long voyages possible: the caravel, a fast ship that could sail against the wind; better maps; and navigation tools like the astrolabe that told sailors their latitude.\n\nPortugal led the way, creeping down the coast of Africa year by year. In 1488, Bartolomeu Dias rounded Africa's southern tip. Then in 1492, Christopher Columbus sailed west for Spain and reached the Caribbean, believing he had found Asia. Soon Spanish and Portuguese ships crossed the Atlantic regularly, and other nations rushed to join the race.\n\nThe crews of these voyages were diverse, and their stories are wider than the famous captains. Estevanico, an enslaved Moroccan man, survived a failed Spanish expedition and then explored the American Southwest as a guide, becoming one of the first Africans to cross North America. He spoke several Native languages and served as an interpreter. His journey shows that the Age of Exploration was not only a European story. Africans, willingly or forced, crossed these oceans too, and their knowledge shaped what the explorers found.",
