        "title": "Life on a Voyage of Discovery",
        "reading": "Imagine months at sea with no land in sight. Ships of the Age of Exploration were small, crowded, and dangerous. Sailors slept on deck or in cramped quarters, ate hard biscuits and salted meat, and drank water that turned foul in wooden barrels. Storms could smash a ship in minutes. Scurvy, a disease caused by lack of fresh fruit, rotted sailors' gums and sapped their strength on long voyages.\n\nShip logs recorded each day's events in brief, factual lines. One entry reads: \"Day 32 at sea. The wind favors us and we made good distance. Two men are sick, but the rest hold their courage. Land must be near, for birds follow the ship.\"\n\nStudy that log entry. The writer notes the wind and distance because those determined survival. He admits two men are sick but stresses courage, perhaps to keep hope alive for whoever reads it. The birds are evidence, read like a clue: land must be close. Sailors learned to read the ocean the way you read a book, watching clouds, birds, and currents for meaning. Behind every famous voyage were hundreds of ordinary sailors whose names history forgot. Their courage, suffering, and skill made the Age of Exploration possible, one logged day at a time.",
