        "title": "Volcanoes: Earth's Pressure Valves",
        "reading": "A volcano is an opening in Earth's crust where magma, hot melted rock, escapes to the surface. When magma reaches the surface, it is called lava. Volcanoes form where tectonic plates pull apart or where one plate dives beneath another, letting magma rise. The shape of a volcano depends on the lava: thick, sticky lava builds steep cones, while runny lava spreads into wide, gentle shields.\n\nThere are three main volcano types. Shield volcanoes, like those in Hawaii, have gentle slopes built by flowing lava. Cinder cone volcanoes are small, steep hills of ash and rock chunks. Stratovolcanoes, like Mount St. Helens, are tall cones built of alternating layers of lava and ash, and they can erupt explosively. Scientists classify each volcano to predict how it might behave.\n\nEruptions can be destructive, burying towns in ash or sending fast flows racing downhill. Yet volcanoes also create. They build new islands, enrich soil with minerals, and released the gases that formed Earth's early atmosphere. Scientists watch these peaks closely, measuring gas and ground swelling to warn nearby towns before an eruption. Like pressure valves, volcanoes release the planet's inner heat. They remind us that the ground we stand on was built by fire.",
