        "title": "The Atlantic Slave Trade: How It Worked",
        "reading": "For more than three centuries, European traders bought and stole millions of Africans and shipped them across the Atlantic. This system is called the Atlantic slave trade. It ran on a cruel triangle: European ships carried guns and cloth to Africa, traded them for captive people, then sailed the Middle Passage to the Americas, where enslaved Africans were sold to work on plantations. The ships returned to Europe loaded with sugar, tobacco, and cotton grown by enslaved labor.\n\nThe Middle Passage was a nightmare of crowding, hunger, and disease, and many captives died before reaching land. Yet even in these terrible conditions, Africans resisted. Some refused to eat. Some rose up against crews. Most held onto their languages, music, and beliefs, keeping their cultures alive across the ocean. That resistance mattered: it meant the trade never fully broke the human spirit it tried to crush.\n\nThe trade enriched European ports and American plantation owners while devastating African societies, which lost millions of young people. It is one of history's greatest crimes, and we study it honestly because forgetting would be worse. The story of the trade is also the story of survival: of people who endured the unendurable and passed down strength, culture, and memory to their descendants.",
