        "title": "How Mountains Are Built",
        "reading": "Mountains are built by the same slow forces that move tectonic plates. When plates collide at convergent boundaries, the crust crumples and folds upward like a rug pushed against a wall. The Himalayas are still rising this way as India pushes into Asia, growing about as fast as your fingernails. When plates pull apart or slide, huge blocks of crust can tilt and lift along faults, forming steep mountain ranges like Nevada's Basin and Range.\n\nVolcanoes build mountains too. Each eruption layers lava and ash higher, and over thousands of eruptions a volcanic peak rises. Some mountains grow from magma pushing up from below without ever erupting, bulging the crust into a dome. Whatever the method, mountain building takes millions of years of patient pressure.\n\nMountains are also worn down even as they rise. Wind, water, and ice grind peaks into sand and carry it to the sea. The Appalachians were once as tall as the Himalayas, but hundreds of millions of years of erosion have worn them into rounded, forested ridges. Every mountain is a balance between building up and wearing down. Like the long struggle for freedom, mountain building shows that the greatest forces work slowly, grain by grain, over ages.",
