        "title": "Empires of Silver and Sugar",
        "reading": "By the 1600s, European nations had carved the Americas into colonial empires. Spain controlled vast territories rich in silver, especially the mines of Potosi in the Andes, where enslaved Native and African laborers worked in brutal conditions. Portugal claimed Brazil and built its wealth on sugar plantations. England, France, and the Netherlands seized islands and coastal strips, each racing for colonies that would make the home country rich.\n\nThe system driving it all was mercantilism, the belief that a nation's wealth came from gold, exports, and colonies. Colonies existed to serve the mother country: they supplied raw materials and bought finished goods. Laws forbade colonists from trading with other nations or making products that competed with home industries. The wealth flowed one way, across the Atlantic to Europe.\n\nBut the colonized did not simply accept this. In Brazil, enslaved Africans who escaped the sugar plantations built Palmares, a great maroon colony of free people in the forests. For nearly a century, Palmares resisted Portuguese attacks, growing to thousands of residents with its own farms, laws, and leaders. It was a colony too, but one built on freedom instead of exploitation. Palmares proves that even inside brutal empires, people built free communities and defended them with courage.",
