        "title": "John Locke and Natural Rights",
        "reading": "The most influential Enlightenment thinker for America was John Locke, an English philosopher. Locke argued that every person is born with natural rights that no government can take away. His words became the blueprint for revolutions: \"All people are born free and equal. Governments exist to protect life, liberty, and property. When a government fails this trust, the people may change it.\"\n\nRead that excerpt slowly. First, Locke says rights belong to people by nature, not as gifts from kings. Second, he defines government's job: protection, not control. Third, he gives the people a power no king had granted them: the right to change a government that betrays its trust. Each sentence builds on the last like steps in a proof.\n\nLocke's ideas traveled across the Atlantic in books and pamphlets. American colonists quoted him when they protested British taxes and laws. Thomas Jefferson borrowed his language for the Declaration of Independence. Yet Locke's own life held a contradiction: he invested in a company involved in slavery. Ideas can outrun the people who speak them. Later generations, including Black abolitionists, would take Locke's logic further than he did and demand that all people truly meant all people. His words outgrew him, as great ideas often do.",
