        "title": "A Revolution Heard Around the World",
        "reading": "The American Revolution was not only an American story. When thirteen British colonies declared independence in 1776, the shock waves reached every continent. France, eager to weaken its British rival, sent money, ships, and soldiers that made American victory possible. Spain and the Netherlands joined the fight. The war became a global contest, with battles fought in the Caribbean, the Mediterranean, and India.\n\nThe revolution's ideas traveled even further than its armies. The claim that people have natural rights and can change unjust governments inspired reformers and rebels from Ireland to Latin America. Enslaved people heard the language of liberty and asked why it did not apply to them. In Virginia, thousands of enslaved people fled to British lines after Britain promised freedom to those who joined its cause.\n\nAmong the revolution's most remarkable figures was James Armistead Lafayette, an enslaved Virginian who volunteered as a spy for the American cause. Posing as a runaway servant, he infiltrated British camps and carried vital intelligence to the Americans, helping to win the decisive victory at Yorktown. He later won his freedom with the help of the Marquis de Lafayette. His story shows that the fight for liberty depended on people the new nation did not yet consider free. The revolution belonged to the world, and the world helped make it.",
