        "title": "The Spark in Saint-Domingue",
        "reading": "In 1791, the French colony of Saint-Domingue was the richest sugar colony in the world. Its wealth came from the labor of nearly half a million enslaved Africans who worked sugar and coffee plantations under brutal conditions. The enslaved workers outnumbered white colonists by more than ten to one. When news of the French Revolution crossed the ocean, its promises of liberty and equality reached everyone, including free people of mixed race who demanded the same rights as white citizens. The colony slid toward open conflict.\n\nOn the night of August 22, 1791, enslaved people across the northern plain rose in a coordinated revolt. They burned plantations and demanded freedom, and the uprising was planned and disciplined. Early leaders included Boukman Dutty, and soon the brilliant Toussaint Louverture rose to command. Louverture had been enslaved, taught himself to read, and studied military strategy. He turned scattered resistance into a disciplined army that defeated Spanish, British, and French forces sent to crush it.\n\nIn a letter to the French government, Louverture wrote: \"We are free by natural right. Do you think men who have known how to be free will let themselves be chained again?\" His words carried the promise of the whole revolution. Freedom, once claimed by people who had earned it, could never be given back. The revolt in Saint-Domingue was becoming something the world had never seen: a revolution led by enslaved people themselves.",
