        "title": "Haiti's Radical Promise",
        "reading": "On January 1, 1804, General Jean-Jacques Dessalines declared the colony independent and renamed it Haiti, taking the name from the island's Indigenous Taino people. It was a world-changing event. Haiti became the first free Black republic in history, the only nation ever founded by a successful revolt of enslaved people, and the second independent nation in the Americas after the United States.\n\nThe promise was radical because it went further than any revolution before it. The American and French revolutions had proclaimed liberty while leaving slavery in place. Haiti's founders abolished slavery completely and wrote a constitution declaring all citizens Black, a direct answer to the racial order of the plantation system. The new nation paid a terrible price for its freedom. France later forced Haiti to pay a huge indemnity for its lost colony, a debt that burdened the country for more than a century.\n\nStill, Haiti's example spread hope and fear across the Atlantic world. Enslaved people in the Americas heard the news and imagined their own freedom, while slaveholding governments tightened their laws in alarm. Toussaint Louverture, captured by the French in 1802, died in a cold prison in France before independence was declared, but his vision lived on in the republic he made possible. The revolution proved that the idea of liberty belonged to everyone, not only to those who already held power.",
