        "title": "Layers of the Sky",
        "reading": "Earth's atmosphere is the thin blanket of gases that makes life possible, and scientists divide it into five layers. The lowest layer, the troposphere, stretches from the ground up to about 7 miles high. Almost all weather happens here, and this layer holds most of the atmosphere's mass. Above it lies the stratosphere, which contains the ozone layer that absorbs harmful ultraviolet light from the sun.\n\nHigher still is the mesosphere, where meteors burn up as shooting stars, and then the thermosphere, where temperatures soar but the air is so thin it would feel freezing to a human visitor. The outermost layer, the exosphere, fades gradually into outer space. Each layer has its own temperature pattern, and together they protect the planet like nested shields.\n\nWhat makes the layers remarkable is how unevenly the air is shared among them. The troposphere alone holds about three quarters of all the air's mass, while the upper layers hold thinner and thinner slices. When you breathe, you are drawing from the densest, lowest layer, the one where clouds form, storms rage, and nearly all living things spend their lives.\n\nScientists measure the layers with weather balloons, aircraft, and satellites. Each tool reaches a different height, and together they build a full picture of the sky above us, from the clouds outside your window to the edge of space.",
