        "title": "Weather or Climate?",
        "reading": "Weather and climate sound alike, but they describe very different things. Weather is what is happening outside right now: today's temperature, clouds, rain, or wind. Climate is the pattern of weather in a place over many years, usually thirty years or more. A single hot day is weather. A city where summers are always hot and humid has a hot, humid climate.\n\nScientists describe climate with averages. They record daily temperatures and rainfall for decades, then compute the mean and median to find the typical conditions. The mean is the average of all the values, while the median is the middle value when the numbers are lined up in order. Both help describe what is normal for a place, smoothing out unusual days.\n\nPensacola, Florida, has a humid subtropical climate: hot summers, mild winters, and rain spread through the year. A visitor checking the weather might see a cool, rainy day in July, but the climate tells the longer story of warm, wet summers. Knowing the difference helps farmers plan crops, builders design homes, and families decide what to pack for a trip.\n\nClimate also changes slowly over time, which is why scientists track decades of data instead of single seasons. A warming climate does not mean every day is hotter; it means the long-term average is shifting upward, one careful measurement at a time.",
