        "title": "Progress and Its Price",
        "reading": "For factory workers, the Industrial Revolution was often brutal. Men, women, and children worked twelve to sixteen hours a day, six days a week, tending dangerous machines for low pay. Children as young as six crawled under moving looms to fix threads. In the fast-growing cities, families crowded into dark tenements with no clean water, and disease spread quickly through the narrow streets.\n\nA young mill worker later testified: \"We worked from five in the morning until eight at night. The machines never stopped, and neither could we. I saw children fall asleep standing up, and the overseers beat us for slowing down.\" Testimonies like this shocked the public and slowly led to laws limiting hours and banning the worst child labor.\n\nYet the machines also created surprising heroes. Elijah McCoy, the son of fugitive slaves who had escaped from Kentucky to Canada, became a brilliant engineer. In 1872 he invented an automatic lubricator that oiled steam engines while they ran, so trains and factory machines no longer had to stop for oiling. His device worked so well that buyers asked for \"the real McCoy,\" and his lubricators kept the machines of the Industrial Revolution running.\n\nOver time, workers organized unions, won shorter days, and earned safer shops. The revolution's price was real, but so was the progress it forced into law.",
