        "title": "Renewable or Not?",
        "reading": "Not all resources are equal. Renewable resources can be replaced as fast as we use them, or they never run out at all. Sunlight, wind, and flowing water are renewable: the sun rises every day whether or not we collect its energy. Trees and crops are renewable too, as long as we replant what we harvest and give them time to regrow.\n\nNonrenewable resources exist in fixed amounts and take millions of years to form. Coal, oil, and natural gas are the famous examples: once burned, they are gone for human purposes. Metals and minerals like gold, copper, and cobalt are also nonrenewable. We can recycle them, but the Earth is not making more on any timescale that matters to us.\n\nThe difference matters for every country's future. Nations rich in sunlight and wind can build energy systems that last forever, while nations depending on coal or oil must plan for the day the mines and wells run dry. Africa holds both kinds of wealth: some of the best solar energy potential on Earth, and vast mineral reserves the world needs for batteries and electronics.\n\nUsing resources wisely means asking two questions: can nature replace this, and how fast? A forest cut carefully can regrow in decades. A barrel of oil burned today took millions of years to form and will never come back.",
