        "title": "China and the Opium Wars",
        "reading": "In the 1800s, Britain had a trade problem with China. British shoppers loved Chinese tea, silk, and porcelain, but China wanted little that Britain made, so British silver flowed east to pay for it all. To fix the imbalance, British traders began selling opium, grown in British-ruled India, to Chinese merchants. The drug spread quickly, addiction soared, and silver began flowing back west.\n\nIn 1839, the Chinese official Lin Zexu seized and destroyed huge stores of British opium at Canton. Britain answered with warships, and in the First Opium War, Britain's steam-powered navy crushed China's older fleet. The Treaty of Nanjing in 1842 forced China to open five ports to British trade, hand over Hong Kong, and pay a large indemnity. It was the first of many unequal treaties.\n\nA second war in the 1850s forced China to open even more ports and grant foreigners special legal rights. Soon Britain, France, Germany, Russia, and Japan had all carved out spheres of influence, zones where each held special power over trade. China was never fully colonized, but for a century foreign powers treated it as a prize to be divided.\n\nThe opium trade showed imperialism at its cruelest: a government pushing an addictive drug on another nation for profit, then using war to protect the trade. China's long struggle against these treaties would shape its history for the next hundred years.",
