        "reading": "Everything you can touch is made of atoms, tiny particles far too small to see. For centuries, scientists believed atoms were the smallest possible pieces of matter. Then, around 1900, experiments revealed that atoms themselves have parts: even smaller particles with their own jobs inside the atom.\n\nAt the center of the atom sits the nucleus, a dense core holding two kinds of particles. Protons carry a positive charge, and the number of protons decides which element the atom is: one proton makes hydrogen, six make carbon, eight make oxygen. Neutrons carry no charge at all, and they help hold the nucleus together. Their number can vary, which is why one element can have slightly heavier or lighter atoms.\n\nAround the nucleus swarm the electrons, tiny particles with a negative charge. Electrons are far lighter than protons or neutrons, and they move in regions called shells, like layers around the core. It is the electrons, especially the outermost ones, that decide how atoms join together, which is why chemistry is really the story of electrons.\n\nThough atoms are unimaginably small, with millions fitting across the width of a hair, their three particles explain the whole material world. The count of protons names the element, the neutrons add weight, and the electrons write the rules of how everything connects.",
