        "title": "The Powder Keg",
        "reading": "By 1914, Europe was a powder keg waiting for a spark. Four long-term forces had been building pressure for decades. First, the alliance system: Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Italy had formed the Triple Alliance, while France, Russia, and Britain had formed the Triple Entente. Each nation promised to defend its partners, so a fight between two countries could quickly drag in them all.\n\nSecond, nationalism filled Europeans with fierce pride and rivalry. Third, militarism: every great power raced to build bigger armies and navies, and generals drew up war plans years in advance. Fourth, imperial rivalry: the scramble for colonies had left bad feelings, especially between France and Germany. In the Balkans, where Austria-Hungary and Russia backed rival peoples, tensions ran hottest of all.\n\nThe spark came on June 28, 1914, in Sarajevo, Bosnia. A young nationalist named Gavrilo Princip assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary and his wife. Austria-Hungary blamed Serbia and issued harsh demands. Russia backed Serbia, Germany backed Austria-Hungary, and within five weeks the alliance system turned a Balkan crisis into a world war.\n\nNone of the leaders expected a long war. Each side believed its armies would win in months, and cheering crowds filled the streets as soldiers marched off. They could not know that the powder keg they had built would explode into four years of slaughter that would remake the world.",
