        "title": "Telegrams and the March to War",
        "reading": "In the last days of July 1914, two cousins tried to stop the war by telegram. Kaiser Wilhelm II of Germany and Czar Nicholas II of Russia, both grandsons of Britain's Queen Victoria, exchanged urgent messages nicknamed the Willy-Nicky telegrams. On July 29, the Czar wired: \"I beg you in the name of our old friendship to do what you can to stop your ally from going too far.\" The Kaiser replied that Germany was doing its best, but the armies were already moving.\n\nThe telegrams failed. Mobilization plans, once started, were almost impossible to stop: millions of soldiers were already boarding trains according to timetables drawn up years before. By August 4, Germany had invaded Belgium, Britain had declared war, and the lights were going out across Europe. Two cousins with every reason to make peace could not halt the machine their own militarism had built.\n\nAcross the Atlantic, Black Americans faced a painful question. The United States would enter the war in 1917 claiming to make the world safe for democracy, yet Black citizens at home faced segregation and lynching. Still, more than 350,000 Black soldiers served, many hoping their sacrifice would earn the democracy they were denied. W.E.B. Du Bois urged them to \"close ranks\" and fight, believing service would force America to keep its promises.",
