        "title": "Why the Czar Fell",
        "reading": "In 1914, Czar Nicholas II ruled Russia as an absolute monarch, with total power over one sixth of the Earth's land. But his empire was brittle. Most Russians were poor peasants, factories were few, and the czar resisted every demand for reform. When World War I began, Nicholas made a fateful choice: he took personal command of the army, tying his own reputation to every defeat.\n\nThe defeats came quickly. Russian soldiers marched to the front without enough rifles, food, or boots, and millions were killed, wounded, or captured. At home, the railways broke down, bread lines stretched through freezing cities, and prices soared. The mysterious holy man Rasputin gained power over the royal family, and rumors of corruption poisoned the czar's name.\n\nIn March 1917, women marching for bread in Petrograd were joined by striking workers and then by soldiers who refused to fire on the crowds. Within days the capital was in revolt, and the czar's generals told him the army would no longer obey. Nicholas abdicated, ending three hundred years of Romanov rule. He had lost a war, his capital, and finally his crown.\n\nThe czar's fall did not bring peace. Power passed to a weak temporary government that kept Russia in the war, and a second, more radical revolution was already gathering in the soviets of workers and soldiers.",
