        "title": "Soviets, Revolution, and a Poet's Journey",
        "reading": "After the czar fell, two powers competed for Russia. The temporary government tried to keep the war going, while the soviets, councils of workers and soldiers, demanded bread, land, and peace. In November 1917, the Bolsheviks led by Vladimir Lenin seized key buildings in Petrograd and took power, promising to end the war and give land to the peasants.\n\nRevolutionary posters spread the new message in bold images. One famous poster shows a giant worker breaking his chains, with the words \"Workers of the world, unite!\" in blazing letters. The worker towers over tiny kings and priests fleeing in fear. Every detail was designed to teach: the old rulers were small and frightened, while the united workers were strong enough to break any chain.\n\nNews of the revolution traveled far, reaching a young Jamaican poet named Claude McKay. In 1922, McKay traveled to Russia, where crowds celebrated him as a voice of the world's oppressed peoples. He wrote about the journey with excitement, seeing in the revolution a promise that colonized and working people everywhere might rise together. His visit linked Harlem's writers to Moscow's revolutionaries in one worldwide story.\n\nMcKay's journey shows how the Russian Revolution echoed beyond Russia. For many around the world, it was proof that even the mightiest rulers could fall when ordinary people united.",
