        "title": "Bond Ratios: Recipes of Chemistry",
        "reading": "Chemical formulas are recipes written in ratios. Table salt is NaCl: one sodium atom for every one chlorine atom, a 1 to 1 ratio. Water is H2O: two hydrogen atoms for every one oxygen atom, a 2 to 1 ratio. Carbon dioxide is CO2: one carbon to two oxygens, also 2 to 1. The ratio never changes, no matter how much of the compound you make.\n\nRatios work like fractions, which makes them easy to scale. If water needs 2 hydrogens for every 1 oxygen, then 4 hydrogens need 2 oxygens, and 10 hydrogens need 5 oxygens. Multiply both sides by the same number and the ratio stays true. Chemists use this trick to figure out how much of each ingredient a reaction needs.\n\nYou can also read a formula backward to find its ratio. MgCl2 has one magnesium and two chlorines, a 1 to 2 ratio. Once you see the pattern, formulas stop being a code to memorize and become simple recipes: count the atoms, write the ratio, and you know exactly how the compound is built.\n\nThis is why the ratio 1 to 2 appears everywhere in chemistry. Water, carbon dioxide, and magnesium chloride all share it, and each one behaves completely differently. Same ratio, different atoms, brand-new substance: that is the quiet magic of bonds.",
