        "title": "Posters, Propaganda, and Ethiopia's Stand",
        "reading": "Dictators ruled minds as well as streets. Their governments flooded daily life with propaganda: posters on every wall, radio speeches in every home, and giant rallies staged like theater. One typical poster shows a muscular worker and farmer marching shoulder to shoulder beneath a giant portrait of the leader, with the slogan \"One People, One Leader, One Future!\" The enemy appears as a shadowy, hook-nosed caricature lurking at the edges.\n\nEvery detail is a trick. The giant leader looks strong and certain while real problems go unmentioned. The simple slogan replaces complicated truth with an easy chant. The ugly caricature teaches viewers to hate a whole group of people. Propaganda works by repeating these images until they feel like common sense, which is why learning to read them critically is a defense of freedom itself.\n\nIn 1935, Mussolini invaded Ethiopia, one of only two independent African nations, using poison gas and bombers against its armies. Emperor Haile Selassie pleaded for help before the League of Nations, warning, \"It is us today. It will be you tomorrow.\" Black Americans heard the call: thousands rallied in Harlem, raised money, and volunteered to fight for Ethiopia. Its resistance became a symbol of dignity that inspired Black freedom movements for decades.",
