        "title": "The pH Scale: Powers of Ten",
        "reading": "The pH scale measures how acidic or basic a solution is, from 0 to 14. A pH of 7 is neutral, like pure water. Numbers below 7 are acids, and numbers above 7 are bases. Stomach acid sits near 2, rain is about 5.6, seawater is about 8.1, and bleach is near 13. The scale fits the whole chemical world into fifteen numbers.\n\nThe pH scale hides a powerful secret: each whole step means ten times the strength. A solution with pH 3 is ten times more acidic than one with pH 4, and one hundred times more acidic than pH 5. These are powers of ten: 10 to the first power is 10, 10 to the second is 100, 10 to the third is 1,000. Small moves on the scale mean huge changes in strength.\n\nDecimals let the scale speak precisely. Rain at pH 5.6 is slightly more acidic than a solution at pH 6.0, and scientists track such small differences to watch for acid rain. Powers of ten and decimals work together: the scale's steps are powers of ten, while the decimals pinpoint exactly where a solution sits between them.\n\nNext time you see a pH number, remember the hidden multiplication. A single point on the scale is a tenfold leap, which is why even small pH changes in lakes or soil can matter so much.",
