        "title": "Speed and Velocity",
        "reading": "Motion is all around you, and speed tells you how fast it happens. Speed equals distance divided by time: if you walk 100 meters in 50 seconds, your speed is 2 meters per second. Common units include meters per second, kilometers per hour, and miles per hour. A car's speedometer simply does this division for you, over and over.\n\nVelocity is speed with a direction attached. Saying \"the train moves at 80 kilometers per hour\" gives its speed. Saying \"the train moves at 80 kilometers per hour north\" gives its velocity. The direction matters: two runners circling a track at the same speed have constantly changing velocities, because their direction keeps turning.\n\nYou can solve any speed problem with one formula: speed equals distance over time. A cyclist covering 30 kilometers in 2 hours moves at 15 kilometers per hour. A plane crossing 3,000 kilometers in 5 hours flies at 600 kilometers per hour. Distance, time, and speed are locked together, and knowing two always reveals the third.\n\nTimelines use the same thinking. The gap between 1933 and 1945 is 12 years, found by subtraction, just as speed is found by division. Whether you measure motion or history, intervals turn dates and distances into understanding.",
