        "title": "The Long Standoff and Africa's Choice",
        "reading": "The Cold War never turned into a direct war between the superpowers, but it burned hot in proxy conflicts. Korea, Vietnam, and Afghanistan became battlefields where Americans and Soviets backed opposite sides. The closest call came in October 1962, when Soviet missiles were discovered in Cuba, ninety miles from Florida. For thirteen days the world held its breath until a secret deal removed the missiles.\n\nIn his 1961 inaugural address, President Kennedy told the world: \"Let every nation know that we shall pay any price and bear any burden to assure the survival of liberty. To those newly freed nations, we pledge our help in their struggle to remain free.\" The speech promised American support, but it also warned rivals that the United States would compete everywhere, including in newly independent Africa.\n\nAfrican leaders knew the game and played it carefully. As colonies won independence in the 1960s, leaders like Kwame Nkrumah of Ghana accepted aid from both Washington and Moscow, refusing to be owned by either side. Some, like Patrice Lumumba of Congo, were destroyed by the rivalry. Africa's independence movements turned the Cold War into a contest for their friendship, and they used it to win schools, roads, and arms.",
