        "title": "Newton's Second and Third Laws",
        "reading": "Newton's second law connects force, mass, and acceleration in one famous equation: force equals mass times acceleration, or F = m times a. It tells us that a bigger push, or force, makes a bigger change in motion. It also tells us that a heavier object, one with more mass, needs a bigger force to speed up the same amount.\n\nYou can feel the second law when you push a shopping cart. An empty cart shoots forward with a small push, but a full cart barely moves with that same push. The full cart has more mass, so it needs more force to get the same acceleration. Engineers use this law to figure out exactly how strong a rocket engine must be to lift its mass off the ground.\n\nNewton's third law says that for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. When you push against a wall, the wall pushes back on you with the same force. A rocket rises because it pushes hot gas downward, and the gas pushes the rocket upward. The forces are always a pair, acting on two different objects.\n\nTogether the three laws explain almost all everyday motion. The first law says motion continues unless a force changes it. The second law says how much a force changes it. The third law reminds us that forces always come in pairs. Newton wrote them more than three hundred years ago, and engineers still use them to build cars, planes, and spacecraft.",
