        "title": "Friction: The Force That Fights Motion",
        "reading": "Friction is the force that resists motion when two surfaces rub against each other. When you drag a heavy box across a rough floor, friction is what makes it hard to move. Even when you are walking, friction between your shoes and the ground is what keeps you from slipping.\n\nFriction comes from tiny bumps on surfaces that catch on each other. No surface is perfectly smooth, not even polished glass. Under a microscope, every surface looks like a mountain range, and those little peaks grind and stick as they slide past one another.\n\nEngineers measure friction with a number called the coefficient of friction. It is a ratio: the friction force divided by the force pressing the surfaces together, which scientists call the normal force. A rough surface like sandpaper has a high coefficient, near 1.0, while smooth ice on steel can be as low as 0.03.\n\nFriction is not always the enemy. Car tires grip the road because of friction, and without it every car would slide like it was on ice. Athletes choose shoes with the right grip for their sport, and engineers design brake pads to turn motion into heat through friction. The trick is knowing when to fight friction and when to use it.",
