        "title": "A War at Home",
        "reading": "While soldiers fought in Vietnam, another battle raged in America itself. The draft lottery chose young men by birthdate, and many felt it was unfair: college students could delay service, while poor and working-class young men, including Black Americans in high numbers, were sent to fight. Protests erupted on college campuses and in city streets.\n\nMartin Luther King Jr., already famous for civil rights, spoke out against the war in 1967. He said: \"I speak as a child of God and brother to the suffering poor of Vietnam. I speak for those whose land is being laid waste.\" He argued that money spent on war should be spent on the poor at home.\n\nSome Americans called protesters unpatriotic. Others said the war was unjust and had to stop. Families were divided, with brothers arguing at dinner tables and friends splitting apart. The nation had never been so torn over a war.\n\nIn the end, the protests helped turn public opinion. Newspapers that once supported the war began to question it, and politicians felt the pressure. The war in Vietnam showed that a democracy's wars are also fought at home, in the hearts and votes of its people.\n\nThe protests changed how America fights wars. After Vietnam, leaders knew the public would not support a long war without clear reasons. News cameras had brought the fighting into living rooms, and citizens demanded honesty from their government. The lesson endures: in a democracy, the people's voice is part of every war.",
