        "title": "Frequency, Wavelength, and Wave Speed",
        "reading": "Wavelength and frequency are linked like partners on a seesaw: when one goes up, the other goes down. If waves pass you very frequently, each wave must be short, with a small wavelength. If they arrive slowly, each one stretches out long. This link holds true for every kind of wave.\n\nWave speed ties them together with a simple formula: wave speed equals frequency times wavelength. A wave with a frequency of 10 hertz and a wavelength of 2 meters travels at 20 meters per second. Change either number, and the speed changes too.\n\nThis formula explains everyday mysteries. Low bass notes from a speaker have long wavelengths, which is why you can hear the thump of a neighbor's music through walls: long waves bend around obstacles. High-pitched sounds have short wavelengths and are easily blocked.\n\nScientists use this math to measure the universe. Astronomers find the speed of distant galaxies by studying light waves stretched by motion. Doctors use ultrasound waves to see inside the body. One simple formula, frequency times wavelength, opens windows into worlds we cannot touch.\n\nTry the formula on sound. Sound travels about 343 meters per second in air. If a note has a frequency of 343 hertz, its wavelength must be 1 meter, because 343 divided by 343 equals 1. Musicians use this math to design concert halls where every seat hears clearly.",
