        "title": "Light: A Wave You Can See",
        "reading": "Light is a wave, but a very strange one. Unlike ocean waves or sound, light needs no material to travel through: it races across empty space at 300,000 kilometers per second. That is why sunlight reaches Earth in just eight minutes, crossing 150 million kilometers of nothing.\n\nWhat we call light is really a small slice of a huge family called the electromagnetic spectrum. From longest wavelength to shortest, the family runs: radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, and gamma rays. All of them are the same kind of wave, differing only in wavelength and frequency.\n\nYour eyes detect only the visible slice, from red light with the longest visible wavelength to violet with the shortest. Bees can see ultraviolet light that is invisible to us, and night-vision goggles detect infrared heat. The world is full of light we cannot see.\n\nEvery member of the spectrum has a job. Radio waves carry music to your car. Microwaves heat your food. X-rays let doctors see your bones. One family of waves, stretching from the longest radio waves to the tiniest gamma rays, connects your kitchen to the stars.\n\nYour eyes are extraordinary detectors. They can sense a single photon, the tiniest packet of light, in a dark room. Yet they miss almost the entire spectrum, from the radio waves carrying this lesson's ideas to the X-rays at the dentist. Technology extends our senses into the invisible.",
