        "title": "Ordering the Spectrum",
        "reading": "Scientists order the electromagnetic spectrum by wavelength, and that order tells a story about energy. Longer wavelength means lower frequency and lower energy. Shorter wavelength means higher frequency and higher energy. It is one smooth slide from gentle radio waves to fierce gamma rays.\n\nRadio waves stretch longer than a football field and carry the least energy. That is why they pass safely through your body and your walls. Microwaves are shorter, with just enough energy to jiggle water molecules and heat your leftovers.\n\nVisible light sits in the middle, and just past violet comes ultraviolet. UV light has enough energy to burn your skin, which is why sunscreen matters. X-rays are shorter still, with enough energy to pass through skin but not bone, letting doctors photograph your skeleton.\n\nGamma rays are the shortest of all, born in nuclear reactions and exploding stars. Ordering the spectrum by wavelength lines up energy from low to high, and that order explains why each kind of wave behaves the way it does.\n\nColors are just wavelengths your eyes can decode. Red light stretches about 700 nanometers from crest to crest, while violet squeezes into about 400. A nanometer is a billionth of a meter, so these waves are unimaginably tiny. Yet their exact size decides whether you see red, green, or blue. When sunlight passes through a prism or raindrops, each wavelength bends by a different amount, painting the familiar rainbow.",
