        "title": "The Decibel Scale",
        "reading": "Loudness is measured in units called decibels, and the scale is trickier than it looks. A whisper measures about 30 decibels, normal conversation about 60, and a rock concert about 110. But each increase of 10 decibels means the sound is actually ten times more intense.\n\nThat means a 100-decibel sound is not just a little louder than an 80-decibel sound: it is one hundred times more intense. The scale is logarithmic, which is a math word for a scale that multiplies instead of adding. Scientists use it because human hearing covers an enormous range, from the faintest rustle to a jet engine.\n\nSounds above 85 decibels can damage hearing over time. A lawn mower runs about 90 decibels, and earbuds at full volume can top 100. That is why concerts hand out earplugs and construction workers wear earmuffs: once hearing is damaged, it does not grow back.\n\nPercent problems pop up with hearing too. If a machine's noise drops from 100 decibels to 90, the intensity falls by 90 percent, even though the number only fell by 10. Understanding the scale helps you protect the only ears you will ever get.\n\nCities now map noise like weather. Planners measure decibels on busy streets and near airports, then design barriers and flight paths to protect neighborhoods. Quieter cities mean healthier people: constant noise raises stress and harms sleep. Measuring sound is the first step to taming it.",
