        "title": "Justice Marshall and the Courts",
        "reading": "The judicial branch's power grew through the courts themselves, and one man helped shape it more than most: Thurgood Marshall. As a lawyer, Marshall argued case after case against segregation, winning 29 of the 32 cases he brought before the Supreme Court. His greatest victory was Brown v. Board of Education in 1954.\n\nIn that decision, the Court declared: \"Separate educational facilities are inherently unequal.\" With those words, the justices struck down segregated schools, ruling that separation itself violated the promise of equal protection. Marshall's careful legal strategy had turned the Constitution's words into real change.\n\nIn 1967, President Lyndon Johnson appointed Marshall to the Supreme Court, making him the first Black justice in American history. For 24 years, Marshall defended the rights of the accused, opposed the death penalty, and protected free speech from the highest bench in the land.\n\nMarshall's life shows checks and balances in action. A lawyer used the courts to check unjust laws, and the courts used the Constitution to check the states. One branch, armed with the nation's founding promises, can move the whole country forward.\n\nMarshall never forgot where he came from. As a student, he was denied admission to the University of Maryland's law school because of his race, so he attended Howard University instead. Years later, he sued Maryland and won, opening its law school to Black students. He spent his life opening doors others had closed.",
