        "title": "Series Circuits: One Path",
        "reading": "In a series circuit, all the parts are connected in a single loop, one after another, like beads on a string. The current has only one path to follow, so the same current flows through every part of the circuit.\n\nOld strings of Christmas lights were wired in series, which is why one burned-out bulb darkened the whole string. Break the loop anywhere, and current stops everywhere. Every bulb depends on all the others.\n\nVoltage is shared in a series circuit. A 9-volt battery lighting three identical bulbs gives each bulb 3 volts, because the voltage divides among the parts. Add more bulbs, and each one gets dimmer, since the same voltage must stretch further.\n\nSeries circuits are simple and cheap, which is why flashlights still use them. But for anything where one failure should not kill everything, engineers choose a different design: the parallel circuit.\n\nTry predicting before you build. If one bulb in a series string burns out, what happens to the rest? Test it with two bulbs and a battery: unscrew one and watch the other go dark. Now you have seen the series rule with your own eyes. Prediction, test, observe: that is the scientific method in a flashlight. Engineers call this a single point of failure, and they avoid it wherever lives depend on the circuit.",
