        "title": "Parallel Circuits: Many Paths",
        "reading": "In a parallel circuit, the parts connect across separate branches, like lanes on a highway. The current splits up, with some flowing down each branch, but every branch connects directly to the battery. Each part gets the full voltage.\n\nYour home is wired in parallel, and that is why one burned-out bulb does not darken the house. Each light, outlet, and appliance has its own branch to the power source. Flip off the kitchen light, and the living room lamp keeps glowing.\n\nBecause each branch gets full voltage, adding parts does not dim the others. Plug in a toaster while the fridge is running, and both get the full 120 volts from the wall. The trade-off is that the battery or power source must supply more total current as branches are added.\n\nComparing the two designs shows real engineering choices. Series is simple and uses less wire, but one break kills everything. Parallel costs more wire but keeps every part independent. Engineers pick the design that fits the job.\n\nYour home's breaker box guards these branches. Each circuit has a breaker that trips if the total current grows too large, preventing overheated wires and fires. That is why the kitchen and bathroom sit on separate circuits: heavy appliances need their own lanes. Safety, like design, is engineered.",
