        "title": "The Long Fight to Vote",
        "reading": "When America was founded, voting was a privilege for few: mostly white men who owned property. But generation after generation, excluded Americans demanded a share of power. Each victory expanded the meaning of \"we the people.\"\n\nWomen led one of the longest fights. For more than seventy years, suffragists marched, went on hunger strikes, and were jailed for demanding the ballot. Their courage finally won the 19th Amendment in 1920, which reads: \"The right of citizens of the United States to vote shall not be denied or abridged by the United States or by any State on account of sex.\"\n\nThose plain words changed the nation overnight. Millions of women voted for the first time, and American democracy became far more truly democratic. The amendment did not end the fight, since many Black women in the South were still blocked by racist laws, but it was a giant step.\n\nRights come with responsibilities. Voting is both a right and a duty: citizens are expected to learn about candidates, show up on Election Day, and respect the results. A right unused is a right that withers.\n\nThe suffragists' tactics were bold for their time. They picketed the White House, the first group ever to do so, and went on hunger strikes in prison. Their courage embarrassed the nation into action. When the 19th Amendment passed, it proved that persistent, peaceful pressure can rewrite the Constitution.",
