        "title": "The Engineering Design Process",
        "reading": "Engineers do not just guess and hope. They follow a cycle called the engineering design process: ask, imagine, plan, create, test, and improve. Each step feeds the next, and the cycle repeats until the design works.\n\nIt starts with asking: what is the problem, and what are the limits? A bridge engineer asks how much weight the bridge must hold and how much money is available. Clear questions lead to clear goals.\n\nNext comes imagining many solutions, not just one. Engineers brainstorm wildly, sketch ideas, and compare them. Then they plan the most promising idea in detail and create a prototype, a first test version built cheaply and quickly.\n\nTesting is where the truth comes out. The prototype is pushed, dropped, or stressed until it breaks or succeeds. Whatever the test reveals, engineers improve the design and test again. Failure is not the end; it is data.\n\nTry the cycle on a paper airplane. Ask: how can it fly farther? Imagine three designs and sketch them. Plan your favorite, create it, and test with three throws. Measure each flight, improve one fold, and test again. You just ran the full engineering design process at your desk. Record every distance in a table so you can compare fairly.",
