        "title": "Shirley Chisholm: Unbought and Unbossed",
        "reading": "In 1968, Shirley Chisholm became the first Black woman elected to Congress, representing Brooklyn, New York. She arrived in Washington with a motto: \"unbought and unbossed.\" She refused to trade her principles for favors, and she spoke bluntly about poverty, racism, and the Vietnam War.\n\nIn 1972, Chisholm did something no Black woman had ever done: she ran for president of the United States. Party leaders told her to wait her turn. She ran anyway, campaigning in 12 states and winning 152 delegates at the Democratic convention. She knew she would not win, but she ran so that others would see it was possible.\n\nChisholm fought for the people Washington ignored: the poor, the young, and minorities. She helped create the food stamp program's expansion and fought for childcare funding. Her question to voters was simple: if not now, when?\n\nHer campaign cracked open a door. Decades later, when Barack Obama won the presidency, he stood on ground Chisholm had cleared. She proved that the ballot belongs to everyone brave enough to claim it.\n\nShe announced her campaign with words for everyone: \"I am not the candidate of black America, although I am black and proud. I am not the candidate of the women's movement of this country, although I am a woman and equally proud of that. I am the candidate of the people.\" She ran for all Americans, not just some.",
