        "reading": "Here is your design challenge: protect a raw egg dropped from shoulder height so it survives unbroken. You may use only simple materials: paper, tape, straws, cotton balls, rubber bands, and a small plastic bag. The egg must be easy to remove after landing, and your container cannot use a parachute bigger than your hand.\n\nStart with the ask step. The problem: cushion the egg so the landing force stays small. The limits: the material list, the drop height, and no giant parachutes. Write your goal in one sentence: \"My container will protect the egg from a shoulder-high drop.\"\n\nNow imagine. Sketch at least three different designs before you build. Maybe a straw cage, a cotton nest in a box, or a crumple zone of paper. Compare your sketches: which spreads the impact over the longest time? Remember, a longer, softer stop means less force on the egg.\n\nPlan your best idea in detail. List every material and how much you will use. Draw where the egg sits and what touches it first on landing. Tomorrow you build and test, so today's plan must be clear enough to follow without guessing.\n\nStudy real engineers for inspiration. Car designers build crumple zones that collapse slowly in a crash, protecting passengers the way your padding protects the egg. Helmets use foam that crushes on impact for the same reason. Your egg container is a tiny crash-safety lab.",
