        "title": "A Connected World",
        "reading": "Look around you, and the whole world is in the room. Your phone was designed in California, built with parts from Japan and Korea, and assembled in China. Your shirt may have been sewn in Bangladesh from cotton grown in Texas. This web of trade is called globalization, and it links every nation.\n\nThe internet tied the knot tighter. A student in Pensacola can video chat with a cousin in the Philippines, watch news from London, and buy shoes from Italy without leaving home. Ideas, money, and culture now cross borders in seconds.\n\nThis interdependence brings both gifts and risks. Trade has lifted millions out of poverty and filled stores with choices. But when one country's factories shut down, shelves go empty on the other side of the world, as the whole planet learned during recent supply chain crises.\n\nGlobal connections also mean shared problems need shared solutions. Climate change, pandemics, and pollution ignore borders, so nations must cooperate to solve them. The world is more connected than ever, and what happens anywhere can touch everyone.\n\nYour own family may live this story. Many families span countries, with video calls replacing long flights home. Money sent across borders supports relatives and whole villages. These personal connections are globalization at its most human: not just trade statistics, but love crossing oceans.",
