        "title": "A Son of Kenya and Kansas",
        "reading": "No life shows global connection better than Barack Obama's. His father was a student from Kenya, in East Africa. His mother was a young woman from Kansas, in America's heartland. Born in Hawaii in 1961, Obama grew up between worlds: Hawaii, Indonesia, and the mainland United States.\n\nAs a young man, he worked as a community organizer in Chicago, helping poor neighborhoods fight for jobs and hope. He went on to Harvard Law School, where he became the first Black president of the prestigious Harvard Law Review. Then he entered politics, winning a seat in the U.S. Senate.\n\nIn 2008, Obama was elected the 44th president of the United States, the first Black president in American history. Crowds around the world celebrated, from Nairobi to Berlin. His story, a son of Kenya and Kansas, seemed to prove that old barriers could fall.\n\nObama's presidency faced wars, a financial crisis, and deep division at home. Yet his journey remains a symbol of our connected age: a boy shaped by three continents who rose to lead one nation, showing how global roots can grow American dreams.\n\nOn election night 2008, the Associated Press reported: \"Barack Obama was elected the nation's first Black president on Tuesday night.\" He told the cheering crowd in Chicago: \"Change has come to America.\" Obama often credited his mother for his values, teaching him to see the world through others' eyes.",
