        "title": "Plan Your Showcase Experiment",
        "reading": "This week is your science showcase: you will run a small experiment of your own choosing and present your results on Friday. Pick a question you can test at home with simple materials. Good questions compare two things, like: which paper airplane design flies farthest, or does warm water dissolve sugar faster than cold water?\n\nWrite your question, then state your hypothesis: what you predict will happen and why. List your materials and write numbered steps anyone could follow. Decide what you will measure and how, using numbers, not just \"it looked better.\"\n\nPlan for fairness. Test each version at least three times and keep everything the same except the one thing you are changing. Record every result in a table as you go, including the tries that flop. Real scientists report all their data.\n\nBy Thursday you should have your data table finished and your conclusion drafted in two sentences: what happened, and what it means. Friday you present. A great showcase teaches your audience something they did not know before.\n\nLook back at this year's science for ideas. You studied motion, energy, waves, circuits, and magnets: any of them can spark a question. The best experiment is one you genuinely wonder about. Curiosity is the fuel; the method is just the engine.",
