        "title": "Run and Present Your Experiment",
        "reading": "Today you run your experiment. Follow your numbered steps exactly, and record every result in your table right away. Do three trials of each version, and do not throw out data that surprises you: the unexpected result is often the most interesting one.\n\nWhen your tests are done, look at your table. Calculate any averages you need, and decide what your data is telling you. Write your conclusion in two sentences: first what happened, then what it means. Then plan your presentation.\n\nA strong presentation has four parts: your question, your method in brief, your results with a graph or chart, and your conclusion. Draw your graph on paper or a poster: label both axes, give it a title, and make it big enough to read from across the room.\n\nPractice once out loud before Friday. Explain it the way you would to a friend, pointing at your graph as you go. Tomorrow you present your results to your family. You are the scientist now, and the data is yours.\n\nRemember that science is a team sport. Share your findings with classmates and listen to theirs. Ask questions about their methods and offer kind, specific feedback. Today's showcase is not a contest: it is a celebration of a year of thinking like scientists.",
